1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,062
Welcome to practicals five and six:

2
00:00:03,062 --> 00:00:04,987
from files to numbers.

3
00:00:04,987 --> 00:00:10,848
We plot an image's pixel distribution and an audio clip's waveform.

4
00:00:11,648 --> 00:00:18,123
Once you load them, an image or an audio clip behaves like any other numeric dataset.

5
00:00:18,123 --> 00:00:26,655
An image is a grid of pixels, height by width by channels, each pixel three numbers from zero to two fifty five.

6
00:00:26,655 --> 00:00:35,491
An audio clip is a list of amplitude samples over time at a fixed sample rate, like twenty two thousand fifty hertz.

7
00:00:35,491 --> 00:00:42,728
So an image has a fixed array shape, and audio is a time series where order and spacing matter.

8
00:00:43,528 --> 00:00:49,203
Step one is to load the file and look at its shape before plotting anything.

9
00:00:49,203 --> 00:01:02,271
Our sample image is four hundred by three hundred pixels, R G B, so the array shape is three hundred, four hundred, three, with values from zero to two fifty five per channel.

10
00:01:02,271 --> 00:01:03,840
The code is one line:

11
00:01:03,840 --> 00:01:06,976
convert the opened image to a numpy array.

12
00:01:07,776 --> 00:01:08,525
Step two:

13
00:01:08,525 --> 00:01:14,930
flatten each colour channel and count how many pixels fall at each intensity.

14
00:01:14,930 --> 00:01:19,671
Each tall spike is one flat-coloured region in the scene:

15
00:01:19,671 --> 00:01:21,917
sky, ground, sun, mountain.

16
00:01:21,917 --> 00:01:31,233
A natural photo usually gives a smoother, more spread-out histogram because real lighting varies pixel to pixel.

17
00:01:31,233 --> 00:01:37,056
In code, convert to greyscale, flatten, and call hist with fifty bins.

18
00:01:37,856 --> 00:01:38,579
Step one:

19
00:01:38,579 --> 00:01:40,106
load the wave file.

20
00:01:40,106 --> 00:01:44,688
Each sample is one amplitude reading at a moment in time.

21
00:01:44,688 --> 00:01:57,628
Our clip has a sample rate of twenty two thousand fifty hertz, lasts two seconds, and has forty four thousand one hundred samples stored as sixteen bit integers.

22
00:01:57,628 --> 00:02:05,665
Build a time axis by dividing the sample index by the sample rate, then plot amplitude against time.

23
00:02:05,665 --> 00:02:08,237
The shape is a plucked envelope:

24
00:02:08,237 --> 00:02:10,568
a quick rise and a slow fade.

25
00:02:11,368 --> 00:02:12,075
Step two:

26
00:02:12,075 --> 00:02:18,207
average the amplitude in small time windows to see the trend the eye picks up.

27
00:02:18,207 --> 00:02:30,156
Bucket the samples into hundred millisecond windows and average the absolute amplitude in each, exactly like a rolling average on any other time series.

28
00:02:30,156 --> 00:02:35,344
This is the same trend concept as monthly sales, applied to sound.

29
00:02:36,144 --> 00:02:39,307
Two new data shapes, same old toolkit.

30
00:02:39,307 --> 00:02:40,056
For both:

31
00:02:40,056 --> 00:02:47,215
load the file, turn it into numbers, check shape and range, and plot the distribution.

32
00:02:47,215 --> 00:02:56,121
For images, use P I L open, then numpy array, then a histogram per channel; peaks mean flat colour regions.

33
00:02:56,121 --> 00:03:08,607
For audio, read the wave file into a one-dimensional array, check sample rate and duration, and plot amplitude over time; the envelope shows loudness.

34
00:03:08,607 --> 00:03:10,272
Thanks for watching.

